# --- Data --------------------------------------------------------------------
# MovieLens may NOT be redistributed (see data/README.md). Fetched, never committed.
data/raw/
data/interim/
data/processed/
data/external/
!data/README.md
!data/assets/
!data/lexicon/

# Figures ARE committed. They are renderings, and matplotlib stamps its version
# into every PNG, so the bytes depend on the plotting library rather than on the
# result: two people regenerating from identical JSON get different files. That
# argued for keeping them out, and for a while they were.
#
# It was the wrong trade. reports/text/main.tex embeds seven of them, so a fresh
# clone built the report with seven "figure not generated" boxes, and the results
# were invisible to anyone reading the repository rather than running it. A
# figure nobody can see is worth less than a figure whose bytes are not
# reproducible. The JSON beside them is the reproducible artefact and the digest
# in each caption ties the image back to it, so the provenance claim survives.
#
# Consequence to know about: a regenerated PNG shows as modified whenever
# matplotlib changes, even with the underlying result identical. Check the digest
# in the caption, not the bytes.
# run_mutation_tests.py edits a module in place, because conftest.py puts the
# working tree's src/ on sys.path and the tests must import the mutant. It writes
# the original here first and restores in a finally. A file matching this pattern
# means a run was killed mid-mutant, and the module beside it may still hold a
# mutation: recover with `python scripts/run_mutation_tests.py --restore <module>`.
# Ignored so a killed run can never be committed, either as a stray file or, far
# worse, as a silently mutated source.
*.mutation-backup

reports/*.csv
!reports/text/

# The experiment JSON *is* committed, deliberately. A figure or a quoted number
# whose underlying data is absent from the tree is an assertion nobody can check,
# which is the one thing this repository is built not to be. Each file carries
# its own result digest and the provenance of the run that produced it, so a
# reader can regenerate it and compare rather than take it on trust.

